        "problem": "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,2y-z=0,\\,x+y=0}.$",
        "worked_solution": "Solving $2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=2y.$ Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-y \\\\ y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}]=y{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{{v}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=1.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
